 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls**
-*   **Parallel Rays**: If the ray direction is perpendicular to the plane normal, the denominator is zero. This must be checked to avoid division by zero [S2, S3, unofficial].
-*   **Back-face Culling**: If the renderer supports back-face culling, check if $D < 0$ (assuming outward-facing normals) and reject if so. Not explicitly mentioned in sources, but common in practice [G].
-*   **Precision**: For rays nearly parallel to the plane, $t$ can become very large, leading to precision issues in the intersection point calculation [G].
+*   **Parallel Rays**: If the ray direction is perpendicular to the plane normal, the denominator is zero, causing division by zero. This must be checked to avoid invalid results [S2, S3, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Bounded Plane Intersection
-Intersects a ray with a finite plane segment (e.g., a rectangle). Requires additional tests to ensure the intersection point lies within the bounds. Choose when you need to limit the plane's extent, such as for a floor tile. See [Axis-Aligned Rectangle Hit Test](5279-axis-aligned-rectangle-hit-test.md).
+Intersects a ray with a finite plane segment [G]. It is distinct from the infinite plane method by requiring additional bounds tests to ensure the intersection point lies within the segment [G]. Choose it when the plane has finite extent [G]. See [Axis-Aligned Rectangle Hit Test](5279-axis-aligned-rectangle-hit-test.md).
 
 ### Infinite Ground Plane
-A specific application of the infinite plane intersection, often used as a base for scenes. It is typically kept separate from the scene's BVH for performance. See [Infinite Ground Plane](#) (link to parent if applicable, or just note it's a usage pattern).
+A specific application of the infinite plane intersection used as a scene base [S1, unofficial]. It is distinct by being kept separate from the scene's BVH for performance [S1, unofficial]. Choose it for ground planes in path tracers [S1, unofficial]. See [Infinite Ground Plane](#).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity**: $O(1)$ constant time. Only a few dot products and a division are required.
-*   **Memory Complexity**: $O(1)$ constant space. No additional data structures are needed.
-*   **Strengths**: Extremely fast, simple to implement, no preprocessing required.
-*   **Limitations**: Only works for infinite planes. Cannot handle bounded planes or complex shapes.
-*   **When to use**: Ideal for ground planes, sky domes, or any infinite surface in a path tracer.
-*   **When not to use**: When the plane has finite bounds or when the scene contains many planes that can be efficiently accelerated with a BVH.
